A week of institutional housekeeping and hard questions: Parliament has waved through the expansion of government to 30 ministries, the 2027 budget has slipped by a week, and two Auditor General findings lay bare how much public money and timber has quietly walked out the door. Meanwhile, a cholera outbreak has reached the railways, and the Junior Chipolopolo are running riot abroad.

## Main Stories

1. **Parliament approves expansion of government to 30 ministries**
Parliament approved the realignment from 24 to 30 ministries, splitting Energy into Electricity and Petroleum, Commerce into Trade and Exports and Industry, Education into Education and Skills and Higher Education, and Youth, Sport and Arts into Youth and Sports and Culture and Arts. This follows President Hichilema's request under Article 92(2)(d), flagged to Parliament just yesterday. The government's efficiency argument is asserted, not evidenced — no costings for the new Cabinet, permanent secretaries and support staff have been published, and the reporting does not say how many of the new portfolios will be filled by sitting ministers.

2. **2027 budget presentation postponed to Nov. 6**
Vice President Mutale Nalumango told Parliament the budget slips a week from Oct. 30 to allow government to "consider additional measures currently being worked on" — measures she did not specify. Analysts note the new 30-ministry structure will likely reshape allocations. This is the first budget under the Grow Zambia Agenda, and the delay is unexplained beyond a vague formula; taxpayers deserve to know what measures are being worked on and by whom.

3. **Govt must account for over 5,000 missing Mukula logs – Mpika MP**
Mpika North NRPUP MP Francis Kapyanga called the 2025 Auditor General's report "a crime scene," demanding investigations into 5,568 confiscated Mukula logs unaccounted for. Records from Mpika District Forestry Office show 5,595 logs confiscated between 2021 and 2025, but a May 2026 physical inspection found only 27 — leaving 5,568 logs whose value "could not be established." This is a genuine follow-up to the Auditor General's findings flagged last week, and the accountability gap is stark: no arrests, no named officials, no explanation of where the logs went.

4. **Govt lost K3.3m due to over 74,000 unrecorded properties – AG**
The Auditor General found the Ministry of Lands failed to collect an estimated K3.3 million in ground rent because it lacked land size details for over 74,000 properties. Only 131,152 certificates of title were issued — 3.3 percent of the targeted four million. The figure is modest in national terms, but the systemic failure is not: a lands registry that cannot bill what it cannot measure will keep leaking revenue indefinitely.

5. **FIC reveals how shell company was paid K85m in govt contract**
The Financial Intelligence Centre's 11th Money Laundering Trends Report discloses a shell company incorporated in 2023 that received K85 million from a public institution between October 2024 and July 2025, then moved roughly K70 million onward within days. It was not registered for relevant tax types and had no record of tax payments, salaries or statutory contributions. The report anonymises the institution as "Public Institution A" — a redaction that protects the guilty as much as the innocent, and one the FIC should be pressed to lift where investigations permit.

6. **Cholera on passenger train as 25 test positive**
A south-bound Zambia Railways train with 500 passengers was quarantined near Mapepe in Chilanga after 25 passengers showed cholera symptoms, with patients aged one to 80 transferred to Chilanga Health Cholera Treatment Centre. Health authorities mobilised 500 vaccine doses, and the Zambia National Public Health Institute reported 68 new cases and one death, bringing cumulative cases to 1,488 with 29 deaths. The outbreak is clearly not contained; the reporting gives no indication of the source of the initial infection beyond Kapiri Mposhi, nor of whether rail sanitation is being inspected.

7. **Police charge soldier with dangerous driving after injuring 2 colleagues**
Warrant Officer Class 1 Christopher Peter Phiri, 52, has been charged with dangerous driving after allegedly failing to keep to his lane and hitting two female Zambia Army officers on guard duty at Apollo Military Camp. Both are admitted to Maina Soko Military Hospital. The report is thin on the obvious questions: was alcohol or speed a factor, and has the Zambia Army opened its own inquiry alongside the police case?

8. **Felix Mutati and Douglas Syakalima nominated and sworn in as MPs**
President Hichilema nominated MDC leader Felix Mutati and former Education Minister Douglas Syakalima as MPs, sworn in by Speaker Nelly Mutti. Mutati's return is politically notable — an opposition leader absorbed into the governing benches — but the report offers no explanation of what role either will play, or whether Mutati's MDC membership survives the nomination.

9. **Don't reduce PAYE in 2027 budget, ZIPAR advises**
ZIPAR Executive Director Zali Chikuba urged government not to cut PAYE, arguing revenue is needed to finance the Grow Zambia Agenda, and called for traction on TAZAMA Open Access. He also defended two supplementary budgets in one year as not necessarily poor planning. That last point deserves scepticism — the article gives his reasoning but no counter-view from anyone who thinks two supplementary budgets in a year is exactly what poor planning looks like.

## Other Notable Stories

**Sports:**
* The Junior Chipolopolo closed their U15 FIFA World Cup group with a 3-2 win over Comoros, after a 13-0 thrashing of Montserrat in which Maxen Msimuko scored seven goals. The campaign included a 3-1 win over Tanzania, a 3-1 loss to Uruguay and a 2-1 win over Moldova — a genuinely impressive run worth celebrating.
* Zambia Athletics bagged four medals at the NamibRe Windhoek City Run, with Brenda Mumbi taking gold in the 21.2km women's race and Jackson Kashiya silver in the men's.
* Monze hosted the Afrivert Energy Monze Motor Rally 2026, round three of the Zambia National Rally Championship, backed by K300,000 in sponsorship over 91.68km of gravel. The MO Drive Racing Team's Geoffrey Chulu and navigator Kapuzya Mbevya took overall honours, with a one-two finish for the team.

**Environment & Agriculture:**
* The Zambia Meteorological Department urged farmers to plant drought-tolerant crops ahead of an anticipated El Niño, following a 14-day outlook warning of 41°C to 44°C temperatures. This echoes the warning flagged a month ago by Prof. Lubinda Haabazoka — the advice is consistent, but the reporting still gives no sense of whether seed for those alternative crops is actually available or affordable.
* Government will inspect private and public premises, including security-sensitive locations, to collect charcoal production data under a World Bank-funded Eastern Province programme, with carbon credits promised to communities that manage forests well. The inclusion of security-sensitive locations is unexplained — a gap that invites suspicion where none may exist.
* Lake Kariba water levels continue their seasonal decline, now at 481.49 metres against 478.31 metres a year ago.

**Justice, Health & Public Service:**
* Former permanent secretary Mwamba Peni has applied for habeas corpus at the Lusaka High Court after his August 21 arrest over a vehicle he allegedly failed to surrender.
* DEC arrested seven suspects for drug trafficking, including an alleged supplier to illegal miners at Danger Hill Mine in Mpika.
* Police in Kitwe arrested a 25-year-old man filmed on social media stealing two 25kg bags of mealie meal from a moving truck in Kandabwe.
* NIPA executive director Prof. Jacob Malungo told 800 public servants in Livingstone to "stop being busy, start delivering," invoking the principles of serve, safeguard and deliver. Fine words — the test is whether the 30-ministry expansion makes delivery easier or merely adds more people to be busy.

**Diplomacy & Investment:**
* Zambia's Ambassador to Japan, Dr. Tobias Mulimbika, courted Japanese investors in Osaka, citing the Japan-Zambia Investment Agreement that entered into force on July 30, 2026, and opportunities in mining, agriculture, energy and tourism. Japan's Ambassador Misako Takahashi said she hopes the agreement improves Zambia's investment climate.

**Politics & Society:**
* President Hichilema donated a bus to the SDA Lusaka Conference youth ministries, fulfilling a pledge made on July 27, 2026 — a small but concrete example of a promise kept.

## Key Takeaways & Watchpoints

* **The 30-ministry structure needs a price tag:** With the budget now due Nov. 6, watch for the cost of the expanded Cabinet and whether the new Electricity, Petroleum, Trade and Exports, and Industry ministries come with new funding or merely reshuffled allocations. The one-week delay should be explained, not just announced.
* **Two Auditor General findings need follow-through:** The 5,568 missing Mukula logs and the 74,000 unbilled properties are documented losses, not allegations. Watch for whether the NPA's new Environmental and Wildlife Crime Department actually prosecutes the Mukula case, and whether the Ministry of Lands publishes a plan to register the missing properties.
* **The FIC's K85 million shell company:** The report anonymises the public institution involved. Watch for whether investigators name it, and whether any prosecution follows — an intelligence report without enforcement is just a paper trail.
* **Cholera is not contained:** With 1,488 cumulative cases and 29 deaths, watch the next ZNPHI updates and whether rail and bus sanitation inspections follow the Mapepe quarantine.